	<key>readme</key>
	<string>## Setup

Run `pwsetup` once to download and verify the pinned Apple extension.
Requires Chrome, Brave, Edge, or Chromium. This edition uses bundled MIT bridge code with Python's standard library; apwcli and chauffeur are not runtime dependencies.

Requires macOS Sequoia or later. No Accessibility permission is needed.

## Usage

* Opening `pass` prewarms the isolated browser without pairing, querying accounts, or reading credentials.
* `pass github [search text]`: use one keyword path, query `github.com` automatically, and show matching password accounts directly in Alfred.
* Full domains and URLs use the same input path; exact aliases keep priority.
* Apple exposes accounts by website. Entries with no Website field cannot be read through its browser helper; an empty bare-keyword search explains this directly.
* `pass codes github`: show verification-code accounts on demand; Return copies a code directly.
* Return on a password account copies it and opens a temporary window for manual typing. Command-Return copies its matching code directly. The Copy Verification Code button in the password window also copies the code and closes the window. Codes never open a value window.
* Option-Return on an account or Set Alias in the password window opens `pass alias save ` in Alfred's own input field. Type a name, check the account preview, and press Return; then use `pass gh-work` to reuse that account.
* Normal alias results use Command-Return for a code and Option-Return to rename.
* `pass alias`: list aliases; Command-Return renames, Option-Return removes an alias.
* `pass alias add gh-work github.com [username]`: bind an alias without copying a secret.
* `pass setup` prepares the extension; `pass lock` closes the active session.
* `pass stats` shows recent average and P95 phase timings without account data.
* `pcode`, `pwsetup`, and `pwlock` remain as compatibility shortcuts.

Account metadata is returned for Alfred's result list. Passwords and codes are never returned to Alfred's debugger or stored in Notification Center. By request, the copied password is displayed in a temporary local window (disable Show password after copy if desired); screen viewers can see it.
Aliases and five-minute editor drafts store account identifiers only, never a password or OTP seed.
Clipboard privacy markers request exclusion from history.
The empty `pass` menu may start the unpaired browser; the first account search starts pairing. Pairing is reused until 30 minutes of inactivity by default, screen lock, sleep/session switch, manual pwlock, or a 30-minute maximum lifetime. Apple may invalidate pairing earlier.
The idle timeout is configurable. A later copy starts a fresh session.

The broker uses a mode-0600 Unix socket and a per-session token in a mode-0700 directory. Passwords never travel back to Alfred over that socket. Browser control still uses anonymous CDP pipes, with no TCP debugging port. These controls do not authenticate Alfred as a unique application or protect against a compromised macOS user account.

The Apple extension is still modified to carry the bridge. A compromised Mac or modified workflow can still access secrets; this is not an independent security audit.

The existing standalone OTP generator needs no extension setup:

Copy a time-based verification code for a stored account via the `otp` keyword.

* &lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Copy the current code.
* &lt;kbd&gt;⌥&lt;/kbd&gt;&lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Forget this account.

Add an account by copying its secret or `otpauth://` link, then using the `otpadd` keyword. Give it a name, or leave it blank to take the name from the link. Your clipboard is cleared once the seed is stored.

The clipboard is cleared after the delay set in the Workflow’s Configuration, but only if it still holds the copied value.

The `otp` command generates codes locally from the stored seed (RFC 6238), independently of Apple Passwords. Use `pcode` for codes in Apple Passwords.
</string>
